---
import ToolLayout from '../components/ToolLayout.astro';
import ConvertTool from '../tools/convert/ConvertTool';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="Convert HEIC, AVIF, SVG, GIF, JPG, PNG, WebP | iDuckImg"
  description="Convert HEIC, AVIF, SVG, GIF and BMP to JPG or PNG, switch between JPG, PNG and WebP, or turn images into PDF. Free, in your browser, no upload and no signup."
  heading="Convert images"
>
  <ConvertTool client:load />
  <section slot="content" class="prose">
    <h2>How to convert an image</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your HEIC, AVIF, SVG, GIF, BMP, JPG, PNG or WebP files, or paste them.</li>
      <li>Choose the format you want: JPG, PNG, WebP or PDF.</li>
      <li>Press Convert and download each file, or all of them as a ZIP.</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>HEIC photos from an iPhone</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm">
      iPhones save photos as HEIC, which many apps and websites cannot open. Choose JPG to get a file
      that works everywhere. The first HEIC file loads a decoder of about 2 MB, which is why it can take a
      moment to start. HEIC is for input only; to make a HEIC you would need a different tool.
    </p>
    <h2 class="mt-6">AVIF, SVG, GIF and BMP</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm">
      These four also work as input, here and on every other picture tool. They are turned into a PNG first, in
      your browser. A GIF keeps only its first frame, so it does not animate. An SVG has no pixel size, so it is
      drawn at least 1024 pixels on its longer side. AVIF needs a browser that can open it (current Chrome, Edge,
      Firefox and Safari 16.4 or later); if yours cannot, you get a message instead of a broken file.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      Converting to JPG fills transparent areas with white because JPG cannot store transparency.
      PDF output here makes one PDF per image. To put several images into a single PDF, use
      <a class="link" href="/image-to-pdf/">Combine images into one PDF</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</ToolLayout>
